Hey @FrancisMurillo! Thanks for your PR, from what I see the code is pretty clean that’s nice!

> Should the parsing of the tasks be cached instead of being parsed for every index since it might add a performance penalty?

We don't mind at all about performance when importing dumps (at least old one), the only thing that matters it to keep the code simple and easily testable thus I would prefer if you keep it like that but thanks for asking 👍 

> Should I assume the data is sorted in some order like with uuid or updateId? I assumed the list is unordered.

The list seems to be sorted, but all the indexes are mixed together.
Which means you’ll typically have things like that;
```json
{ "index_uid": "a", "task_uid": 0 }
{ "index_uid": "a", "task_uid": 1 }
{ "index_uid": "a", "task_uid": 2 }
{ "index_uid": "b", "task_uid": 0 }
{ "index_uid": "a", "task_uid": 3 }
{ "index_uid": "b", "task_uid": 1 }
{ "index_uid": "b", "task_uid": 2 }
```

> I was planning to populate my dev instance with data and dump my data. Is there a way to dump with previous versions?

The only way to create an old dump is to run an old version of meilisearch and create a dump sadly.
I already took some time to craft a dump for each version of meilisearch so you don’t have though. It doesn’t test every possible things but it’s still pretty good.
You should find the dump-v3 version here; `MeiliSearch/dump/tests/assets/v3.dump`
And since the dump are just a `.tar.gz` you can actually extract it to see its content if you want with the following question; `tar xzf v3.dump`

